WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
What do you actually own when
you own a share of stock?

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:05.760
Let's start with one pizza shop.

00:00:06.503 --> 00:00:07.953
Rosa owns a pizza shop.

00:00:08.643 --> 00:00:10.053
Every dollar of profit is hers.

00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:13.103
She wants a second shop, which would cost

00:00:13.153 --> 00:00:15.773
$100,000, and she doesn't have the cash.

00:00:16.223 --> 00:00:19.583
She could borrow it, but then
she'll owe the bank, and she'll have

00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.993
to pay back in good years and
bad, which is quite risky.

00:00:23.443 --> 00:00:26.413
Or she could sell part of her pizza shop.

00:00:27.057 --> 00:00:28.047
So she does.

00:00:28.097 --> 00:00:30.527
Let's picture the
company as one big pizza.

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.177
Rosa cuts it into 80 equal slices, and

00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:35.207
at the start, every slice is hers.

00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:38.647
Then the company cuts 20 new slices and

00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:41.467
sells them to a neighbor for $5,000 each.

00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:44.307
That's $100,000, and it goes to her

00:00:44.357 --> 00:00:46.627
company, which builds the second shop.

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:51.099
Now, there are 100 slices, and
the neighbor holds 20 of them.

00:00:51.609 --> 00:00:53.259
Those slices are called shares.

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:57.299
20 shares out of 100 is 20%
of Rosa's Pizza, a fifth of

00:00:57.349 --> 00:01:01.339
every future profit, and a fifth
of the votes on big decisions.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:06.899
Years later, suppose Rosa's Pizza
has 40 shops and wants 200 more.

00:01:07.389 --> 00:01:08.859
Maybe it goes public.

00:01:09.349 --> 00:01:12.629
In an initial public offering,
it cuts many more slices

00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:15.959
of the company, and banks
find investors to buy them.

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:18.999
The money from that sale
goes to the company.

00:01:19.452 --> 00:01:22.592
After that, the shares can
trade on a stock exchange,

00:01:22.642 --> 00:01:25.782
and the company is no longer
involved in trading them.

00:01:26.132 --> 00:01:29.282
When you buy a share, you're
buying it from another investor.

00:01:29.852 --> 00:01:33.042
Airbnb sold new shares at $68 in 2020.

00:01:33.672 --> 00:01:36.342
The next day, stock buyers and sellers

00:01:36.392 --> 00:01:39.062
were trading the shares at about $145.

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:41.056
What sets the price?

00:01:41.866 --> 00:01:45.136
A slice is worth its share
of every future profit.

00:01:45.506 --> 00:01:48.706
Nobody knows those profits
for sure, so the price is the

00:01:48.756 --> 00:01:51.956
market's best guess, and it moves
whenever that guess changes.

00:01:52.732 --> 00:01:53.862
Every three months,

00:01:54.612 --> 00:01:56.762
Rosa's Pizza reports its profits. If it's

00:01:56.812 --> 00:01:58.962
a public company, that's required by law.

00:01:59.792 --> 00:02:04.482
Say investors expected profit to
grow 20%, and it grew just 10%.

00:02:04.532 --> 00:02:08.792
The company made more money than
last year, but the stock will

00:02:08.842 --> 00:02:13.102
still fall because the price had
already counted on 20% growth.

00:02:13.752 --> 00:02:17.322
Prices move on the gap between what
happened and what was expected.

00:02:18.075 --> 00:02:21.665
If you have a retirement account
at work, you'll probably end up

00:02:21.715 --> 00:02:25.305
owning slices of hundreds of companies
like this one, through a fund.

00:02:25.675 --> 00:02:29.195
You won't necessarily pick the slices.
You'll own a little of everything, and

00:02:29.245 --> 00:02:32.765
you'll get a share of profits
of every company in the market.
